From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [PATCH v2 0/3] Thread each revision under the one before
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 08:01:19 +0000

Each revision of a series has opened a thread of its own, so the
versions of one series and their review are spread across the list.
This series posts each revision as a reply to the version before it,
its cover letter or its only patch. Every version stays in one
thread, and the versions form a spine down it, each one beneath the
review it answers.

This is git.git's convention before June 2026, and b4's
"send-same-thread = yes". git.git now replies to v1 instead, to keep
lore's web view shallow; the research is in "Re: [PATCH 1/3]
libexec/mail: Thread each revision under its first version" [1].
That concern does not apply to a list read in notmuch.

Patch 1 makes format-patch.sh pass --previous as --in-reply-to.
Patches 2 and 3 bring lab-process-mail, the mail workflow guide, and
freebsd-port in line. The Link to v(N-1) line stays, since it names
the previous version in the text.

The series needs "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised
series" [2], which adds --previous; its patch ids are in the
prerequisite-patch-id lines below. It applies to b/dev@upstream.

I tested patch 1 by formatting a v3 of an existing single patch.
With --previous as a bare Message-ID, as id:<...>, or as <...>, the
patch replies to that message. An explicit --in-reply-to overrides
it, and a v1 replies to nothing.

base-branch: b/dev@upstream

Changes in v2:
- Reply to v(N-1) rather than v1, as Pat asked in review [3]. The
  walk up In-Reply-To to v1, and its helpers, are gone.
- Describe the spine as this list's rule and cite git.git's
  convention before June, rather than "as git.git does".
- Drop the patch-triage hunk, since b/dev moved that skill to
  src/claude-skills-old; patch 3 now touches only freebsd-port.
- Drop the note on the superseded tag, which is withdrawn.
- This cover replies to v1's cover by hand. format-patch.sh could not
  rebuild v1 for the interdiff: v1 has prerequisite patches, and they
  are not on b/dev. The range-diff below compares v1 rebuilt on its
  original base with v2.
